00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.500
to take all of you developers
through the learning curve,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.370
and today we will build a neural
network together from scratch

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:59.190
to something that I believe is
a good quality neural network.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.069
And if some of you have seen
other talks from the TensorFlow

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:07.290
Without a PhD
series, today we will

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.260
be focusing on the latest
advances in visual processing

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.300
neural networks and what are
the architecture ideas that

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.860
go into a good
neural network today.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.510
So let's start, and for
that we need a dataset.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.420
So what can we do?

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.980
Let's head to Kaggle, the
data science community.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.520
I went there, and I found
this dataset of little 20

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.380
by 20 tiles with airplanes
and non-airplanes.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.400
Actually plane spotting
was quite a nice activity.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.400
Why don't we build something
that can recognize airplanes

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.610
from not airplanes and then
continue building an airplane

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.860
detection neural network.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.390
So how do we start?

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.320
Well, let's take the
neural network manual 101

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.690
on the first page and show--

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:02.520
the first page, it says this
is a fully connected or dense

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.500
neural network.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.810
So the image comes in
as a set of pixels,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.360
and we flatten all the
pixels into one big vector.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.870
And we will be processing
that big vector.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.420
The white circles
you see are neurons.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.320
So a neuron in a neural network
will always do the same thing.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.640
A neuron does a weighted
sum of all of its inputs

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.060
and then feeds the sum through
what is called an activation

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.200
function.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.990
That's just a function,
number in, number out.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.770
But in neural networks,
this activation function

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.120
is always non-linear.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.520
This is what-- this is the key
thanks to which neural networks

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.710
can solve non-linear problems.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.940
So you can layer those
layers of neurons.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.530
The second layer, instead of
doing weighted sums of pixels,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:57.420
does weighted sums of the
outputs of the previous layer.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.200
You could have as many
layers as you want.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.920
And then at the very
end, I added a layer

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.900
with just two neurons.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.740
And here, since I'm building
a classifier, something

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.110
that will classify those
images into airplane,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:15.520
non-airplane, my hope is that
with the correct weights,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.240
these two neurons, one of them
will have a very strong output

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.610
when this is a plane,
and the other one

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:26.320
will have a very strong
output if this is not a plane.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.920
So we will need to choose the
activation functions correctly.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.900
And again looking at the
neural network 101 manual,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.400
most of the time the
activation function you use

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.260
is called a ReLU.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.950
And the manual says if
you're building a classifier,

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.320
there is one exception
on the very last layer.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.700
You will use a different
activation function,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.370
which is called softmax.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:54.190
So I will dive into those
two on the next slide,

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.590
but first let's write
the code for this.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.620
So this is what it looks
like in TensorFlow.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.650
The first line just
flattens all the pixels

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.930
as one big vector of pixels.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.780
And then in TensorFlow, you
have this high level API,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.680
which can instantiate an
entire layer in one line.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.620
So here I have instantiated
my three layers.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.790
The first one with 200 neurons
then 20 and the last one

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:20.709
with two neurons.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.630
As you see the
intermediate layers

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.900
are activated with this
ReLU activation function.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.330
And at the end, I need to
do something different.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.450
So if you are
building a classifier,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:32.860
let's follow the recipe.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.770
The recipe says if you're
building a classifier,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:40.110
the last layer must have the
softmax activation function,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.760
and you will apply--

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.590
you will compute
now some distance

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:50.290
between what your
network is predicting

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.940
and the correct answer.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.750
You-- we are doing here
supervised training.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.704
So we don't know initially
what those weights are.

00:05:00.704 --> 00:05:02.370
We are doing weighted
sums, but we don't

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.780
know what those weights are.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.250
And we will be feeding
examples into this model where

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.380
we know the answers and
comparing what the model is

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.850
predicting against our answers.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.420
So we need a distance function.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.420
And in a typical classifier,
the distance you will use

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.150
is called cross entropy.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.190
I don't even
necessarily need to know

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.050
at this point what that is.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.330
TensorFlow actually
has a function--

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:30.950
a combined function--

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:34.430
that applies this lost
softmax activation

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:37.610
and computes the
cross entropy distance

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.950
between what the
network predicts

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.990
and the correct answer.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.300
This is called a
loss error function,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:48.060
and that's what we
want to minimize.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.050
So as soon as you
have built your layers

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:55.320
and computed your loss,
an error of function,

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.500
TensorFlow can take over.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.630
You pick any of
the optimizers that

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.480
are available-- here I
chose Adam optimizer--

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.710
and you kindly ask it
to minimize this loss.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.720
This will give you a
training operation,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.660
which TensorFlow will
then repeat in a loop,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.640
and what happens in this
training operation is

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.790
all the training magic.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:19.550
TensorFlow will look at your
loss, will differentiate it

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.080
relatively to all the losses--

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.630
sorry, to all the
weights in the system,

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:27.350
obtain something that is called
a gradient mathematically,

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:31.220
and apply an algorithm
gradient descent

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:36.230
that figures out how to change
the weights in your network

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:40.650
so as to minimize this loss--
so as to make this loss smaller.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.560
And so you will be
feeding in images

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.950
and correct answers in batches.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.540
And at each batch,
TensorFlow slightly

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.630
adjusts the weights
in your network

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:54.470
so as to make the loss,
the distance between what

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:57.620
your network is predicting and
the correct answer, smaller

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.360
and smaller and smaller.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.940
That is how you train
a neural network.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.710
So just a little look at those
two activation functions.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.160
So here is again just
one neuron, weighted sums

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.050
of all of its inputs.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.950
Usually you add
something called a bias.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.640
That's another degree of
freedom, something that

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.010
will be determined by training.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:22.080
And you feed this through
an activation function.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.960
So this ReLU
function that we have

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.150
used on the intermediate layers,
this is what it looks like.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.420
It's very, very
simple function, 0

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.820
for all negative
values, identity

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.610
for all positive values.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.680
The softmax activation function
that you use on the last layer,

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.680
here I have represented the
last year with 10 neurons,

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.960
so that's if you're
doing a classification

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.920
into 10 categories.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.790
So that softmax function
is slightly more complex.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:51.470
Well, actually it's
just an exponential,

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.780
so you compute a weighted
sum and elevate it

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:54.980
to the exponential.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.920
And then you normalize
across those 10 neurons.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.860
Well, the output--

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.480
I put in a little animation.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.220
Here is what it does.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.600
Boom.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.840
It pulls the winner apart.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.840
But without destroying--
it's not max.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.530
It doesn't completely
nullify all the bad answers.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:18.350
So you still have
signal if your network

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:20.750
is mis-recognizing something.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:22.310
That's why it's called softmax.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:24.380
It's max but in a soft way.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:29.120
It pulls the winner apart,
but it doesn't discard all the

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.670
bad-- all the negative
information, which

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.980
is still useful for training.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.380
So we have all that.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.280
We have built our network.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.380
Now we can train it.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.179
We have our dataset.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:43.669
During training--
well, so here is

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:46.240
what goes in the witch's brew.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.050
So ReLU on softmax,
the two activation

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:53.330
functions, the cross entropy was
the distance function we used.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:56.240
And it's ready.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.190
Let's test.

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:04.580
Actually before we go there,
a word about the tooling

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:05.850
we're using here.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.750
So this is-- the code
is in TensorFlow.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.570
The tool I'm using to
actually run the training

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.030
is ML Engine on Google's Cloud.

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:17.450
It has one super
useful feature that I

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.570
love is that whatever
infrastructure

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:25.280
I run my training on, when the
job is done, it shuts it down.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.960
That's not rocket
science, but I just

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.620
can't be bothered shutting
down my machines because--

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.920
and figuring out of
my jobs I've done.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.050
ML Engine gives me
the job-based view.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.990
I can show you here
that I need to work

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.850
my jobs running finished.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:44.480
And then when they are finished,
the machine or the cluster

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.510
goes down.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.100
And finally TensorBoard,
that's a visualization tool

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:52.140
that you have in TensorFlow
where you see all your curves

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:55.610
and you can see what's going on.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:59.240
So I will-- oh, no.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:00.545
I can't skip this.

00:10:00.545 --> 00:10:05.090
When handling images--
so I will have

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:08.450
to introduce another
piece of technology here.

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:11.600
Those dense neural networks
that you have seen,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.670
they work well for
a lot of things,

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.260
but for images you
need something else.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:20.130
And those are called
convolutional neural networks.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.770
So bear with me here.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:25.370
In a convolutional neural
network, what is called

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.430
a neuron behaves a
little bit different.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:31.400
A neuron-- this little cube
here, that's the output--

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.360
sees only a little fraction
of the image right above him.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.630
It doesn't do weighted sums
of all the pixel of the image,

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.940
just a little portion.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:45.470
And then the next
neuron actually does

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:49.490
a weighted sum of a little
portion just above him

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.220
but using the same weights.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.980
So it's actually a
filtering operation.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.740
You pick a set of
weights, as many weights

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:01.310
as I have highlighted cubes
in the image over there.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.710
My image has the red,
green, and blue channels

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:05.360
because it's a color image.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.680
So if you have red,
green, and blue channels.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.630
And I'm doing weighted
sums of all these pixels

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:14.810
but using the same
weights at each position.

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:17.030
It's a filtering operation.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:22.310
And once I have moved this
filter across my entire image

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.450
with correct padding
on the sides,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:31.600
I have as many outputs as
I had pixels initially.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.010
So how many weights did I use?

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.620
Well, as many as the
highlighted pixels.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:40.760
So that's 4 by 4 by 3,
which is 4 by 4 16, 48--

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.940
48 weights.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.160
Typical neural
networks have something

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.040
in the tens or hundreds
of thousands of weights.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:53.350
So we need a way of giving
this more freedom, more weights

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:54.780
to play with.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:58.240
And a good way is to pick
another set of weights

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.160
and repeat the operation.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.890
So you pick just another set of
weights, repeat the operation,

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:06.730
and you obtain a new channel
of data in the output.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:10.160
And you can do that
again and again,

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.900
which means that a convolutional
layer in a neural network

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.790
will transform a data cube
into another data cube.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:22.060
And this is the shape
of this convolutional--

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.900
the matrix of weights.

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.370
So the first two digits--

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.200
the first two numbers 4 by 4--

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:29.860
that's the size of
the filter in pixels.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:34.300
The third digit is how many
channels of information

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:38.500
here you are reading in the
input image so three channels

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.430
red, green, blue.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:41.240
That's this number here.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.870
And then you repeat this
operation four times

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.530
with four different sets of
weights, and as a result,

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.020
you obtain four different
channels of outputs.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.720
And the four is this
last number here.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.500
So this is a
convolutional layer.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.540
And since it has a
number of input channels

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:02.500
and a number of output
channels, you can chain them.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.260
And a convolutional
network will be a sequence

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.140
of those layers
transforming the data

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.975
cube into another cube
then filtering that data

00:13:10.975 --> 00:13:16.280
cube again, transforming it
into a new data cube and so on.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:20.030
So this data cube can
grow in both directions,

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.660
the vertical direction or
the horizontal direction.

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:26.410
So we have seen here in the
vertical direction, that's

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.080
just the number of times
you repeat the filtering

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:37.640
operation with a
different set of weights.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:41.960
But how do you adjust the size
in the horizontal direction?

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.660
Usually the idea is to
go from an image and boil

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.640
the information
down into something

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:50.090
smaller like recognizing
what is in the image.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:51.920
So two choices
for that actually.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:53.430
We have many options.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:57.200
The first one is to play with
the step of the convolution.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:01.000
Instead of doing those
weighted sums pixel by pixel,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.980
you jump every second pixel.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:07.010
Well, mechanically you
obtain twice fewer results

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:08.270
in the output.

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:10.610
That's the stride
parameter that you

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:14.090
see here, stride 2 or stride 1.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:20.900
And there is a second option,
which is actually more used,

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:22.940
and that's called max pulling.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:25.100
And here the idea
is interesting.

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:27.200
These are filtering operations.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:32.760
So as the network
trains, you will--

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:36.630
those filters will
train to pattern match

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:39.554
or recognize certain
features in the image.

00:14:39.554 --> 00:14:40.970
Let's say there
is one that trains

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:44.370
to recognize little horizontal
lines, another one that

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:47.320
specializes in vertical
lines, and so on.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.950
So the output of the
filter is basically

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:53.010
something like here I have
seen a little horizontal line.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.510
Here I have seen nothing.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:57.120
Here I have seen
nothing and so on.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:02.130
The max pulling operation
takes four of those in a square

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.980
and just keeps the
max, which makes sense

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.790
because you are interested in
the value of the filter that

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.500
was maximum at that
point because that

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.470
is where the filters has
actually seen something.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:15.940
The other ones, which
again-- which you discard,

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:19.630
are I've seen nothing and
that's not really interesting.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:22.380
So this is a basic
subsampling operation.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:23.490
You take your image.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.310
You take all-- well, not your
image but your data cube.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:32.880
You take the data points
4 by 4 and squares 2 by 2

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.260
and just keep the maximum.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.028
And again that reduces the size
of the data cube horizontally.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:43.230
And the little guy, he
points something out.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:47.150
That is something called
a 1 by 1 convolution.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.280
Well, if you're a
mathematician, that

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:52.670
doesn't make much sense,
like 1 by 1 filter, that's

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:54.980
just multiplying by a constant.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.660
That's not very useful.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:16:00.380
But again we are doing this
filtering multiple times

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.240
with different constants.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:06.200
So 1 by 1 convolution
actually makes sense.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:13.020
It's the weighted sum of this
little column of data points.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.590
And that weighted sum
might be interesting.

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:18.770
So we will see later that 1
by 1 convolutions actually

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:22.850
in convolution
networks make sense.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:24.430
So this is what we will build.

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:26.170
There are three
convolutional layers.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:30.070
We have our image here, three
convolutional layers, and then

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:32.020
at the end, we need
to connect this

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:35.890
to our softmax layer,
which will do the airplane,

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:37.970
non-airplane classification.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.630
So the last data
cube, we reshape it.

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:45.010
We flatten it out as
one big vector here.

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:50.290
And this-- we apply normal
dense layers to this vector

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:54.550
and end up with our
softmax, softmax activation,

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:59.680
and softmax cross entropy loss
because this is a classifier.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:03.250
After that, a lot
of experimentation

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:05.710
and a lot of regularization.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.880
So I will not go into this.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.849
You have many other
talks in the TensorFlow

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:14.859
Without a PhD series that focus
on what is regularization.

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:17.260
For now, all you
need to know these

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.240
are techniques--
standard techniques that

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:22.060
can improve the convergence.

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:25.750
And I am quite good
at those techniques.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:31.150
So plus another one, which
is hyperparameter tuning.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:33.970
As you have seen, there
are many parameters here.

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:36.100
The number-- the
size of the filters,

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:39.130
the number of
layers, the strides,

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:42.980
and so on and so forth.

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:46.690
You can-- well, if you
know what you're doing,

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:51.190
you know the acceptable
ranges for those parameters,

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.970
but still it's a lot of work to
explore this parameter space.

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:59.710
So that's why ML Engine gives
you this hyperparameter tuning

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:06.070
module where you just define
your space and say go ahead.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.850
Try all the combinations.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.790
But there are a couple
of ways of trying out

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:12.070
all the combinations.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:13.990
The basic one is
just grid search,

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:15.880
and that's where
you would start,

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:18.940
just map out all the
possible combinations

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:22.270
of parameters and search
through the whole grid.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:26.110
It's actually slightly
faster to do a random search

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:27.460
than a grid search.

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:28.870
That's a bit counter-intuitive.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:30.040
We are rational people.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:33.580
I like the grid, but it turns
out random is slightly better.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:36.460
But ML Engine does
a third algorithm

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:38.760
that is called
Bayesian optimization,

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.000
and I won't go into this one.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:46.120
It's something where,
from one set of runs,

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:50.560
it can mathematically determine
which part of the parameter

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:55.240
space has been mapped and where
it's still missing information

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.190
and focus on that other
part of the parameter

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.680
space in an optimal way.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:03.550
So that is the best way of
doing hyperparameter tuning

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:06.360
and ML Engine does that.

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:10.470
How well-- this is how
you package your files

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:15.000
to go to a ML Engine,
just your Python code

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.000
in a folder and a config
file, which usually

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:21.870
has just this in it
scaled here basic GPU,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.290
which means one machine with
the GPU, and then you go run.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:26.520
The run here.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.910
You use the G Cloud command
line, G Cloud, ML Engine, job

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:34.170
submit training and you train.

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:41.490
If you add these lines here to
the config file, you start--

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:44.040
instead of starting a
normal training job,

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.800
you start a
hyperparameter tuning job.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:48.300
So what do you--
what did I do here?

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:51.520
I said I want to
maximize some metric.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:55.200
That is a way of specifying
what your metrics are

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.900
in your TensorFlow code,
so here my accuracy.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:01.620
I want 50 trials, 10
trials in parallel,

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.030
again Bayesian optimization.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:09.510
So it derives useful information
from trials for the next ones.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.310
So it's better not to run all
the trials at the same time,

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:15.000
even if you have the
necessary hardware.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.930
And then you say which
parameters you want.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.080
So I have one
parameter called LR2,

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:22.350
which is an integer, min
max values, the scale.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:25.800
This other one is a
categorical parameter,

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:31.080
and it will try all those
parameters in some optimal way.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:35.520
So using all this, the
network you have seen here,

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:42.200
this one plus my best knowledge
of regularization techniques

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.700
plus hyperparameter
tuning, I was

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:48.900
able to bring this network
to an accuracy of 99.6%,

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:51.570
and I was very,
very proud of myself

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:55.950
until I tried this
network in real life.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:58.200
Let me show you a demo.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.210
Well, first of all
just a little trick,

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:03.537
how do you transform a
classifier into a detector?

00:21:03.537 --> 00:21:04.620
It's actually fairly easy.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.500
If you have a big image,
just cut it out in 20

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:10.770
by 20 tiles slightly
overlapping and maybe

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.860
a different resolution, which
you resize then to 20/20,

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:14.910
run the classifier.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.830
Wherever you see a plane,
you put a box there,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.300
and you have a detector.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:20.810
So here we have--

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:24.840
that's San Francisco-- and
this was my very first model.

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:30.270
This one, not yet very
good, just the data

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:35.250
I had, not much regularization,
not much hyperparameter tuning.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:38.300
I guess it looks like
it's doing something.

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:40.120
It's not completely horrible.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.300
So it's encouraging.

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:46.300
Then I use my best skills
to hyperparameter tune

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:48.600
and regularize the
hell out of this.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:53.880
And I obtained my 99.6%
accuracy model, which I will now

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:56.140
run for you and wait for it.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:03.120
It's absolutely horrible.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.460
You see here-- let me
show you lots and lots

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:13.330
of false positive everywhere.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:14.570
It's noisy.

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:16.060
It's not clean.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:21.000
So here is the first
lesson of neural networks.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.750
There is your training data.

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.680
There is your
evaluation data on which

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:28.420
you compute your accuracy.

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:30.840
Of course, computing your
accuracy on your training data

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:32.370
would be cheating.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:35.460
And then there is real life.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.130
And real life has nothing to
do with either your training

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.070
or your evaluation data.

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:43.020
Real life is hard.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:46.840
So with a lot of effort
actually augmenting the dataset,

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.365
adding tiles of
non-planes, hoping

00:22:49.365 --> 00:22:52.110
that this would
make things better,

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:56.280
I was able to
increase the accuracy

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:58.900
of this model a little bit.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:04.650
But as you will see,
it's still not great.

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:10.950
And that was the end of my
neural network 101 handbook.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:13.425
So from now on, you've
got to read papers.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.400
That's scary, so
before we go there,

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:26.870
let me give you a couple
of just TensorFlow tips.

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:31.040
To use ML Engine really to
the best of its capabilities,

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:33.830
I advise you to wrap
your model into what

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:36.980
is called an estimator API.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.770
That's just because
in an estimator,

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:43.490
we have written for you a
ton of boilerplate coach that

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:47.180
is not interesting to write,
things like check points--

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.770
regularly outputting
check points

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.410
so that if your training
crashes after 24 hours,

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:55.280
you can restart
from where you were,

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:58.190
exporting the model at the end
so that you have something that

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:03.460
is ready to deploy to a serving
infrastructure or distributed

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:04.280
training.

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:06.860
The distributional algorithms
of distributed training

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:09.990
also baked in into estimator.

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:16.720
And to wrap your model in an
estimator, you need those--

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:20.400
sorry-- those four things here.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:23.390
And then you can run
train and evaluate,

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:26.240
which will alternate
training in evaluation phases

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.360
so that in your output, you get
nice curves with your training

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:31.800
data, training metrics,
evaluation metrics.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:35.630
You can compare
the two and so on.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:40.440
Mostly what you need to provide
are those four functions here.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.110
So let's go quickly
through them.

00:24:42.110 --> 00:24:46.980
It's really nothing fancy.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.500
The model function,
it's your layers.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:50.550
That's your model.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:52.110
And then it returns
whatever a model

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.850
is supposed to return,
the predictions the last.

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:59.516
You put the loss
into an optimizer,

00:24:59.516 --> 00:25:01.140
and you've got this
training operation,

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:03.960
which the estimator
will run in a loop,

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:08.140
and whatever evaluation
metrics you care about.

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:10.110
So that's your model.

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:12.600
Then the training
input function--

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:14.960
and I'm putting code
on the slides here.

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:16.980
You don't try to
read all the code.

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:20.880
I will give you the highlights
of what is there in the code.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.740
You will not be-- you
will not have the time

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.090
to see all the syntax.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.790
So the training input
function, that's

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:28.620
the function that
will define how

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:31.110
your data goes into the model.

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:35.074
And I use this dataset API.

00:25:35.074 --> 00:25:36.740
And that's really
good because this data

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:40.080
set API is designed for
out of memory datasets.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:44.640
You define what your dataset
is, and then as you--

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.940
as your model is training,
the data is loaded,

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:50.250
and the loading triggers--

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.890
the loading of additional
files from disk

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:56.130
if the dataset does
not fit in memory.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.220
And by the way, no
dataset ever fits

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:03.660
in memory, no real dataset.

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:07.830
So here, for example, I'm
reading images and focus

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:09.060
on this here.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:11.460
The dataset is
initialized from files,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.170
get matching files
in a directory,

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:15.060
all the files in a directory.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:16.980
And then I like this syntax.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:20.100
It's really the
workflow I'm used to.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.960
I apply some loading
operations, which

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.680
will load those files and
decompress them and so on.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:29.400
I usually need to shuffle
my data into batches

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.000
because the training
always proceeds by batches

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.190
and usually are
repeated indefinitely.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:38.930
And my dataset is done.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.520
And finally, the
serving input function.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:47.470
So estimator also
saves periodically

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:51.610
a snapshot of your model, which
is ready to be deployed, again,

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:52.360
on ML Engine.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.320
ML Engine has those two parts.

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:55.270
One is for training.

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:57.400
The second one allows
you with one click

00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:59.790
to put your model
behind REST API.

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:02.200
But for that, when
your model will

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:04.450
be listening behind
this REST API,

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:06.550
it will be receiving
data in a certain format,

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:10.990
and usually you wants to do
stuff with that before feeding

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:12.770
it into your model.

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:15.790
So if you don't, this
is the do nothing pass

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:18.890
through serving input function.

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:22.010
But if you do, this is the
serving input function,

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:25.510
which I used to first
decompress the incoming images

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.190
from JPEG to pixels.

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:31.570
And then I also
implemented right in this

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:33.280
the scanning operation.

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:37.090
You remember to transform
a classifier to detector,

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:38.810
you need to cut your
image into little 20

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:42.250
by 20 tiles overlapping
at different resolutions.

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:45.100
Actually I was able
to do this on the fly

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:46.690
in the deployed model.

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:48.820
This is the code, so
it's about 10 lines.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:49.900
Don't read it.

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:52.030
But I find it
interesting that I was

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:55.390
able to do this on the
fly in the deployed model.

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.550
And the demo I was
showing here, this

00:27:57.550 --> 00:28:01.210
is a JavaScript UI, which
is calling into ML Engine,

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:04.180
sending a part of the
image to ML Engine

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:07.850
and then getting
the results back.

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:09.048
So this is actually live.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:16.420
So we need to read papers.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:18.760
There are many papers.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:21.730
For detection and image--

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.610
image work and detection,
there are many papers.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:28.890
I want to talk about these two.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:30.330
And a little bit
about the others

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:33.000
but mostly focus
on the big ideas.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:34.170
And the first-- oh, yes.

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:36.540
And I need a new
dataset unfortunately

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:38.550
because now I will be
doing real detection.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:42.000
Those 20 by 20 tiles of
planes and airplanes,

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:43.300
that's not good any longer.

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:44.730
Now I have to handle--

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:48.270
I want to handle big images
indirectly output a square box

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:50.370
around each airplane.

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.110
So I had to build
my own dataset.

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:56.700
In this case, it was
actually possible.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:59.520
I built myself a
little JavaScript UI,

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.790
and then I went
clicking on airplanes.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:06.630
And, well, I-- and it
was about a day of work,

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:10.340
so not so bad here.

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:12.160
So let's start
with an inception.

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:17.330
This is the paper that
really brought to the table

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:20.290
some of the big
ideas in this space.

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:25.340
On this side, you see what the
inception model looks like.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:28.660
So all those little squares are
called in convolutional layers.

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:31.510
And what you see
is that it's weird.

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:34.660
I told you before that
convolutional layers

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:37.690
should be sequenced just piled
up one layer, next layer,

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:39.190
next layer, next layer.

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:43.350
And here we see branches
and then things coming back.

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:45.340
What is that?

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:48.370
So the big idea here is
that you are somewhere

00:29:48.370 --> 00:29:50.560
in your convolutional
neural network.

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:53.110
And then you ask
yourself the question.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.430
What is the best here?

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:57.370
Should I now add a 1 by
1 convolutional layer

00:29:57.370 --> 00:30:02.020
or maybe a 1 by 1 followed by a
3 by 3 or maybe something else?

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:03.850
What is best?

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:06.310
They have the idea
that you could actually

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:08.920
do all of these
things in parallel

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:14.380
and simply concatenate in the
vertical direction the results.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:16.510
And they call this a module.

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:18.580
Basically during
training, the network

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:21.610
will decide which
is the best path

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:25.820
to use for a specific
image on a specific task.

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:27.850
So this is this
module-based approach.

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:30.830
That was one of the big ideas.

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:34.190
The second by big idea is
called filtering factorization.

00:30:34.190 --> 00:30:35.830
So what you see here are--

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:39.550
is a sequence of
two 3 by 3 filters.

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:42.730
You see-- let's look
at this bottom one.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:48.250
So this piece of data is used
by some weighted sum of this 3

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:50.890
by 3 square here.

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:54.370
And if you look where those
the data points in this 3

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:57.070
by 3 square are coming
from, they actually

00:30:57.070 --> 00:31:02.340
come from some combinations
of this white data

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:10.170
points in this 5 by 5
piece of the previous data.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:13.920
So it looks like
two consecutive 3

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:17.880
by 3 filters do some
combinations of a 5

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:20.880
by 5 zone in the same
way as a 5 by 5 filter

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:26.160
will be doing combinations of
data points in 5 by 5 zone.

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:29.070
It's not the same combinations.

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:30.960
But let's count the weights.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:35.070
A 5 by 5 filter has
5 by 5 by 1 by 1.

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:39.030
That's the def-- the
number of channels.

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.180
That's 25 weights.

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:43.470
And now if we count the
weights for three-- two

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:48.575
consecutive 3 by 3 filters, it's
3 by 3 plus 3 by 3 which is 18.

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:54.580
Two 3 by 3 filters
are 30% cheaper

00:31:54.580 --> 00:31:58.995
in terms of number of weights
than one big 5 by 5 filter.

00:31:58.995 --> 00:32:00.370
They're not doing
the same thing,

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:02.990
but it's worth checking
if for our purpose

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:05.782
it wouldn't be enough.

00:32:05.782 --> 00:32:08.760
So that's the second big idea.

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.100
And the third big
idea are those 1

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:13.740
by 1 convolutions,
which again could

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:16.260
sound funny to a mathematician.

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:20.490
But once you realize that
you are applying many of them

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:23.100
and that this 1 by 1
convolution is actually

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.280
a weighted sum of all
of the pieces of data

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:29.620
in this little column,
it's like saying, well,

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:31.830
I have many different
filtering results.

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:34.380
I have filtered my image for
horizontal lines and then

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.426
vertical lines and so on.

00:32:36.426 --> 00:32:37.800
And maybe the
feature I'm looking

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.650
for is some combination
of those filters.

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:43.380
Sometimes I'm looking for
only the horizontal line,

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:45.130
a little bit of
the vertical ones.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:50.830
The 1 by 1 convolution can
give me the right combination

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:52.750
for that purpose.

00:32:52.750 --> 00:32:55.954
Of course, the weights
defining the combination

00:32:55.954 --> 00:32:56.620
will be trained.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:02.700
And one more.

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:05.820
This one is a bit of cheating.

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:08.230
So at the very end, you do
your convolutional layers,

00:33:08.230 --> 00:33:11.720
and at the very end, you
want to classify if you're

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:13.200
building a classifier.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:15.470
So let's say classify
in five classes,

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:19.820
typically you would take the
last data cube here, reshape it

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:22.820
into a vector,
apply a dense layer,

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:26.510
and then softmax activation, and
you've got your five classes.

00:33:26.510 --> 00:33:31.220
But if all you're trying to
do is obtain five numbers,

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:32.570
there is an easier way.

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:35.590
Those dense layers, they connect
everything with everything.

00:33:35.590 --> 00:33:38.100
They tend to be heavy
in terms of weights.

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:39.230
There is an easier way.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:41.010
You need five numbers.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:44.480
Let's take this cube
of data to slice it up

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:48.110
horizontally like a piece
of bread in five slices,

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.380
average those five slices,
and you've got five numbers.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:54.750
You can apply softmax
activation on it if you want.

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:58.610
And you got the same
thing with zero weights.

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:00.980
So it's a lot cheaper.

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:05.200
But warning, it
only works if you

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:08.409
care about global information
and only global information.

00:34:08.409 --> 00:34:10.690
If you want to classify
an image as dog

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:14.530
versus cat versus sunset,
yeah, this will work.

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:17.480
If you actually care about
the localization of stuff

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:21.679
in your image, like we do, you
are averaging your filtering

00:34:21.679 --> 00:34:23.500
output across the entire image.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:29.260
So you will lose
that information.

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:35.440
So not good for our purpose here
but still an interesting idea.

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:38.650
To put this all together
into a simple architecture,

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:42.790
the inception architecture is
a bit complicated for my taste.

00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:44.650
But this Squeezenet
paper actually

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:47.830
put all of this in a very
elegant architecture.

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:52.750
They said let's build everyone
out of these kinds of modules.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:55.449
So we start with a
1 by 1 convolution.

00:34:55.449 --> 00:34:58.885
What we usually reduce
the horizontal size

00:34:58.885 --> 00:35:00.580
of our data cube.

00:35:00.580 --> 00:35:02.630
And then we have
a parallel module,

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:05.140
which is 1 by 1
convolution and a 3

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:07.030
by 3 convolution in parallel.

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:09.640
We stack the results,
and the stacking usually

00:35:09.640 --> 00:35:15.670
increases the number of
channels in the data cube again.

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:19.300
So they call it a
squeeze and expand layer.

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:21.760
And when you put
those two together,

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:23.950
they call them fire modules.

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:27.580
And your full model
becomes a nice succession

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.400
of fire modules, max
spooling operations,

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:35.170
which decrease the size of
your data cube horizontally.

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:37.200
Then again, a sequence
of fire modules

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:40.540
then you decrease the size
again horizontally and so on.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:43.890
It's simple, and
I find it elegant.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.920
So let's build it.

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:49.330
But I wanted to test
this Squeezenet idea

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:53.870
against a more traditional
layer, layer, layer, layer,

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:57.220
layer architecture.

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:01.540
And we'll go into the YOLO
paper, You Look Only Once.

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:02.920
That's the detection
paper, which

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:09.130
we'll use to actually produce
our squares around airplanes.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:12.850
In that paper, they had this
very simple architecture

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:15.940
that they called Darknet.

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:18.540
And I thought, well, maybe
we can compare the two.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:23.300
So you see on the Squeezenet
side, we have this--

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:28.290
these squeeze modules
here-- sorry, no, here--

00:36:28.290 --> 00:36:31.950
1 by 1 followed by in
parallel 3 by 3 and 1

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:35.100
by 1 convolution and then
the max pulling operation

00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:36.450
and then it continues.

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:39.120
So you see the data cube
is a small one squeezed,

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:41.700
then it's expanded then it's
squeezed then it's expanded,

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:43.455
and so on.

00:36:43.455 --> 00:36:46.500
On the other side,
it's just a sequence.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:48.960
And actually the
depth of the cube

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:52.200
is roughly constant
until the end.

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:53.970
And we just have
these max pulling

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:58.570
operations, which restrict the
size of the image horizontally.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:02.230
So we'll see which
one feels better.

00:37:02.230 --> 00:37:06.400
Now how do we actually
detect airplanes?

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.100
This is what the
YOLO algorithm does,

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:10.570
and it's You Look Only Once.

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:12.190
It's a one-shot detector.

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:14.710
It's not You Live Only Once.

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:17.680
It divides the
image into a grid.

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:21.640
And it will say, well, each
grid cell will now produce

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:23.780
a certain number of boxes.

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:27.100
So each grid cell will
be designed to output

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:31.720
four values, which is xy,
the center of the box--

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:33.490
the yellow box here.

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:35.910
And the center can be
anywhere in the grid cell,

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:39.190
so x and y are
between minus 1 and 1

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:41.900
relatively to the
center of the grid cell.

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:46.630
And it will also compute
the size of this box.

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:48.070
The size can be anything.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:52.300
So the box can grow to the-- to
be as big as the image itself.

00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:55.210
It's not constrained
in the grid cell.

00:37:55.210 --> 00:38:00.250
And also some confidence
factor, which tells us whether--

00:38:00.250 --> 00:38:04.404
if there is a plane here or not.

00:38:04.404 --> 00:38:08.520
And you can adjust how
many of those boxes

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.350
each grid cell is
able to generate--

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:11.850
1, 2, 3.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:13.170
We will see what's best.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:18.900
This is the loss they
have in their paper.

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:27.820
So let me-- please allow me
to simplify it a little bit.

00:38:27.820 --> 00:38:29.710
The first line
actually is kind of OK.

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.610
It's the error on the
position of the box.

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:35.620
So you compare the box you
have against your ground truth,

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:37.410
and you see this a squared--

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.400
square of differences
of the center,

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:40.900
so that's the error
on the position.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:42.290
I like that.

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:45.400
The second one is the error
on the size of the box.

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:47.140
Well they had rectangles.

00:38:47.140 --> 00:38:48.650
I have only squares.

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.590
So let's kill the
hate only with.

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:53.500
And for some reason
they thought it was--

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:55.840
in the paper, they
argue why they

00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:59.600
put in the square root of the
size and not the size itself.

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:01.972
I didn't understand
that, so I removed it.

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:06.230
The next thing is the error.

00:39:06.230 --> 00:39:09.850
So this is on
detected airplanes.

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:11.450
What is the conference--

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:13.150
the error on the
confidence factor

00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:15.550
where you had an airplane.

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:18.680
Here I just replaced
the ideal value by 1.

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:22.330
When you have an airplane,
the ideal confidence is 1.

00:39:22.330 --> 00:39:26.020
The next line is the
same thing for boxes

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:27.550
where you do not
have an airplane,

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:30.460
so there the ideal is 0.

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:33.340
And the last line, they were
detecting many categories.

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:36.035
They were actually
detecting cars and dogs

00:39:36.035 --> 00:39:36.910
and blah, blah, blah.

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:38.980
I have only airplanes.

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:42.610
The last line for them was
the missed detection error,

00:39:42.610 --> 00:39:45.200
detecting a car as a dog.

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:47.290
I have only airplanes
or not airplanes.

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:49.716
I don't care about this.

00:39:49.716 --> 00:39:53.710
They also-- so this is
a multi-composite loss.

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:56.800
So they had this idea that
maybe the different parts

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.770
of this loss should be
weighted differently.

00:39:59.770 --> 00:40:02.770
Again, I didn't understand
why, so I removed them.

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:04.620
And that's about it.

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:06.400
So the only thing
that is still left--

00:40:06.400 --> 00:40:08.350
and that is a hard problem--

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:11.650
is this operator
they called one,

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.950
which is actually the
assignments between the boxes

00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:17.390
you generate in
the ground truth.

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:19.150
And that's not an easy problem.

00:40:19.150 --> 00:40:22.180
So, of course,
per grid cell, you

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:24.940
know that if you generate--
if you have a ground truth

00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:28.470
box that is centered in
one of those grid cells,

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:33.280
you will want to pair it with
some box generated by that grid

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:34.420
cell.

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:38.380
But those grid cells are allowed
to generate more than one box.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:41.260
And you're allowed to have more
than one airplane in a grid

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:41.890
cell.

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:44.990
So you might have more
than one ground truth box.

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:48.110
How do you do the pairings?

00:40:48.110 --> 00:40:49.880
Well, there is a lot of choice.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:52.330
And when I looked in
the paper, they said,

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:54.910
well, we did something simple,
and actually our algorithm

00:40:54.910 --> 00:40:57.700
is not very good for
swarm type things

00:40:57.700 --> 00:41:01.600
when you have a flock
of birds or an airport

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:02.380
full of airplanes.

00:41:02.380 --> 00:41:05.060
It's not very good for that.

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:07.670
Thank you, guys.

00:41:07.670 --> 00:41:09.230
So, well, we'll see.

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:12.570
We'll try to play
with these parameters.

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:14.060
How do we build this?

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:16.430
So very simply
you've got the end

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:17.690
of your convolutional network.

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:19.520
You've got a data cube.

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:23.120
Split it horizontally
in your end-by-end grid,

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:27.420
and then split it vertically
in four or maybe eight or 12,

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:32.180
depending on how many boxes you
want to generate per grid cell.

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:35.280
And then the red boxes will
become x, the yellow boxes

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:37.580
y, the green boxes--

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:40.250
the size of the box
and the blue ones

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:42.500
will become the
confidence factor.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:44.750
You just average all
the values in them.

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:47.390
And for all of
those that you need

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:49.850
to put between
minus 1 and one, you

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:53.330
feed them through a hyperbolic
tangent that puts them

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:54.905
between minus 1 and 1.

00:41:54.905 --> 00:41:57.030
And all those that you need
to put between 0 and 1,

00:41:57.030 --> 00:41:58.738
you feed them through
a sigmoid, and that

00:41:58.738 --> 00:42:00.440
puts them between 0 and 1.

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:04.260
And you have generated
from an image

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:11.360
1, 2, 3, 4 boxes per grid
cell, which detect airplanes.

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:11.970
That's it.

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:13.940
We're done.

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:18.530
The only thing now is the
work of the data scientist.

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:22.310
Now starts a slow
but steady process

00:42:22.310 --> 00:42:27.430
of grinding through all the
hyperparameters of this model

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:31.000
and trying to
improve the accuracy.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:35.520
Initially, this is what my
first training run looked like.

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:37.870
Actually let me show
you my real numbers

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:41.480
because I have them here.

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.440
So this is initially what I had.

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:49.120
I wasn't sure it was actually
doing something at all.

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:50.800
But why not.

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:53.860
Initially I tried a 4
by 4 grid and generating

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:55.900
just one box per grid cell.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:56.980
So not very good.

00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:59.770
I quickly realized that
shuffling data is actually

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:02.320
super important.

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:05.960
Big progress in-- so IOU, that's
the intersection over union.

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:09.190
It's a measure of how
accurate those boxes are

00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:12.230
relatively to the ground truth.

00:43:12.230 --> 00:43:16.090
So I tried with more
boxes per cell like 4 by 4

00:43:16.090 --> 00:43:19.540
but generating four
boxes per grid cell.

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:21.250
That doesn't work so well.

00:43:21.250 --> 00:43:24.220
Again, it's a hard problem to
assign them to their ground

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:25.870
truth control counterparts.

00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:28.900
You've got four boxes per
cell, four ground truths.

00:43:28.900 --> 00:43:30.490
Which one is which?

00:43:30.490 --> 00:43:31.000
It's hard.

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:32.660
So this was not very good.

00:43:32.660 --> 00:43:34.960
So then I went to 8 by
8 and then generating

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:36.850
only one box per cell.

00:43:36.850 --> 00:43:39.351
Yeah, that's better because
there is no assignment problem

00:43:39.351 --> 00:43:39.850
there.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:40.974
It's just one box per cell.

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:47.490
I bumped it up to 16
by 16 by 2, the grid--

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:48.790
the YOLO grid.

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:51.430
That is even better,
and this time I

00:43:51.430 --> 00:43:54.010
tried to think very hard
about this assignment problem

00:43:54.010 --> 00:43:58.750
and devise an algorithm
that would rationally

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:00.400
do the pairings.

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:02.810
I think I went by the
distance from the center,

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:07.300
so the first box is
more susceptible to go

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:10.420
with airplanes, which are
closer to the center of the grid

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:15.050
cell and the second one
more on the periphery.

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:21.970
Then it turned out
that data augmentation,

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:25.350
varying at random the hue and
the orientation of the images,

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:27.730
that actually helps a lot.

00:44:27.730 --> 00:44:31.890
And finally, that loss waiting
idea, the multi-part loss,

00:44:31.890 --> 00:44:35.280
figuring out that some
parts should weigh more,

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:37.560
it was actually a good idea.

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:41.880
And that gave me a little
bit more accuracy as well.

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:45.870
And on here you can see in
the reverse order, the loss--

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:48.850
so the error function
going down as we do this.

00:44:48.850 --> 00:44:51.940
And finally the best one
was with more layers.

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:53.850
So these were worth 12 layers.

00:44:53.850 --> 00:44:56.360
The last one is worth 17 layers.

00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:58.760
And then when we got something.

00:44:58.760 --> 00:45:03.030
So you want to see
this in a demo right?

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:04.890
Let's go.

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:07.260
First model, so
this is what we had,

00:45:07.260 --> 00:45:10.200
the best model obtained
from the classifier.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:13.930
Now let's try our first
16-16 counted by by 2,

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:19.020
so that's the YOLO grid, your
catch images in 16 by 16 grid,

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:23.790
and you generate two
boxes per grid cell.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:24.510
Let's go.

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:26.940
Analyze.

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:30.660
It's much cleaner, but it's
missing a lot of planes here.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:37.020
This is something you can solve
with the data augmentation.

00:45:37.020 --> 00:45:40.200
Actually I did not have
any data augmentation here.

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:43.110
By varying the
orientation of my cells

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:46.220
and the hue of the
cells randomly,

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:51.550
you got a big boost
in inaccuracy.

00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:52.990
Now we're talking.

00:45:52.990 --> 00:45:54.850
Now it looks like a detector.

00:45:54.850 --> 00:45:57.430
We still have some
false positives.

00:45:57.430 --> 00:45:58.960
There is one here.

00:45:58.960 --> 00:46:03.010
I saw a couple of other ones,
but it is not so bad actually.

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:08.240
And if we jump to
a bigger model,

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:11.840
I think that from
12 to 17 layers--

00:46:11.840 --> 00:46:14.550
I think we are pretty good.

00:46:14.550 --> 00:46:15.770
Let's see if we are perfect.

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:18.570
We'll see.

00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:19.790
Almost perfect.

00:46:19.790 --> 00:46:23.580
Well, it missed this
blue airplane here,

00:46:23.580 --> 00:46:29.070
but that's because I need more
detail with blue airplanes.

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:31.450
But it's not so bad.

00:46:31.450 --> 00:46:35.070
And I was able to
piece this together

00:46:35.070 --> 00:46:39.750
with LEGO bricks, which appear
in literature without being

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:42.960
a super specialist
in machine learning.

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:48.180
And my message to you here
is that it's kind of hard,

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.980
but it's not like
fluid dynamics hard.

00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:56.140
If you are a good
developer, this

00:46:56.140 --> 00:46:58.510
is a learning curve
through which you can go.

00:46:58.510 --> 00:47:00.940
I went through this
learning curve and look.

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:04.840
My airplane detection
model now works.

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:09.550
It will always have an accuracy
of 99-point-something percent.

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:11.200
You can never go to 100.

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:15.580
That's something you have
to build into your use

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:17.770
case, your product.

00:47:17.770 --> 00:47:20.620
But you-- all of you here
are capable of building

00:47:20.620 --> 00:47:24.020
machine-learning models.

00:47:24.020 --> 00:47:26.510
Then just to finish,
I want to show you

00:47:26.510 --> 00:47:29.120
the tooling I was using.

00:47:29.120 --> 00:47:31.660
So I'm using ML Engine.

00:47:31.660 --> 00:47:34.980
ML Engine has basically
two great features.

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:35.900
One is My Jobs.

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:37.520
I see all my jobs running.

00:47:37.520 --> 00:47:39.450
And the other one is My Models.

00:47:39.450 --> 00:47:42.020
So with one click, I can
deploy a model to production,

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:47.210
and it's served behind a
REST API with auto scaling.

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:49.340
And I don't have
to manage anything.

00:47:49.340 --> 00:47:50.840
I really love that.

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:54.230
I love my job
tuning those models.

00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:57.170
I do not like the
job of chasing VMs

00:47:57.170 --> 00:48:02.055
and which one is still running
and why blah, blah, blah.

00:48:02.055 --> 00:48:07.280
ML Engine, once you wrap your
model in this estimator API,

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:09.910
it gives you a
one-line config access

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:13.640
to many different
hardware architectures,

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:15.301
including TPUs actually.

00:48:15.301 --> 00:48:16.800
But let's start
with something else.

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:20.850
So this is what I had initially
training on just one GPU,

00:48:20.850 --> 00:48:24.610
and this is the config
file that you use for that.

00:48:24.610 --> 00:48:27.970
Scaled here, basic GPU,
that's one machine with a GPU.

00:48:27.970 --> 00:48:31.080
This model, the 17-layer
model, the bigger one,

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:35.930
trains in 23 hours
for a cost of $28.

00:48:35.930 --> 00:48:37.440
We have faster GPUs.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:39.610
You can bring this
down to 10 hours

00:48:39.610 --> 00:48:42.860
like that for the same price.

00:48:42.860 --> 00:48:47.230
But with a very-- just a config
change, no change in your code,

00:48:47.230 --> 00:48:51.100
you can actually deploy this to
full cluster of five machines

00:48:51.100 --> 00:48:52.870
with five GPUs each.

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:59.080
And it's a cluster, so you can
go to 100 if your model scales.

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:01.570
With this one I'm down
to 4 and 1/2 hours.

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:04.450
That's-- that means
much more productive.

00:49:04.450 --> 00:49:07.990
And with the better GPUs,
that actually below two hours.

00:49:07.990 --> 00:49:12.460
I wish I had that in
the very beginning.

00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:17.109
But then I thought, well,
let's try those TPU things.

00:49:17.109 --> 00:49:18.400
First of all, a little warning.

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:20.140
Those TPUs, it's
new architecture.

00:49:20.140 --> 00:49:21.880
There is a little bit
of a porting effort

00:49:21.880 --> 00:49:23.950
to adapt your model to TPUs.

00:49:23.950 --> 00:49:26.440
We are working on that,
but right now expect

00:49:26.440 --> 00:49:29.650
to spend some time
tweaking the code.

00:49:29.650 --> 00:49:32.510
It will still work on
GPUs once you tweaked it,

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:34.960
but there are some
things that need to be

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:37.260
done for this new architecture.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:40.610
It's a completely new chip.

00:49:40.610 --> 00:49:41.420
Oh sorry.

00:49:41.420 --> 00:49:45.680
Before that, new in TensorFlow
are those distribution

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:46.970
strategies.

00:49:46.970 --> 00:49:49.760
So very soon-- as soon as
they ship-- it's in beta now--

00:49:49.760 --> 00:49:54.170
you will be able to reserve one
box with multiple GPUs in it,

00:49:54.170 --> 00:49:55.550
and that's a
different trade off.

00:49:55.550 --> 00:49:59.390
So you don't have the network
communication between the GPUs,

00:49:59.390 --> 00:50:02.660
but on the other hand, you can
put 100 of them in one box.

00:50:02.660 --> 00:50:04.790
So it's a different trade off.

00:50:04.790 --> 00:50:07.790
And again, it will be available
with just a conflict change.

00:50:07.790 --> 00:50:08.630
So now on to TPUs.

00:50:11.810 --> 00:50:14.060
This is available.

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:16.460
I just ported it,
so I don't want yet

00:50:16.460 --> 00:50:18.410
to show the numbers
because it's not

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:25.460
really benchmark with a
proper benchmarking set up.

00:50:25.460 --> 00:50:28.700
But those numbers
are not secret.

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:31.250
Cloud TPUs are all available
on ML Engine today,

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:33.990
and I published a code
for this yesterday.

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:36.720
So all you have to do is run it,
and you will see the numbers.

00:50:36.720 --> 00:50:39.053
And, of course, if they are--
if I am putting them here,

00:50:39.053 --> 00:50:42.140
it's because the are
more-- faster and cheaper

00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:47.300
than the second best option
you have here on this slide.

00:50:47.300 --> 00:50:50.810
And very soon, you will
be able to access not just

00:50:50.810 --> 00:50:56.840
one of those TPU boards but 64
boards so a full rack of them

00:50:56.840 --> 00:50:58.970
connected with a
high-speed interconnect

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:03.320
and use all of that as
one big supercomputer

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:07.510
straight from ML Engine
with just a conflict change.

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:09.490
If you want to know
more about this,

00:51:09.490 --> 00:51:13.670
there is a session exactly on
TPUs right after this session,

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:15.339
but you'll have to
cross the street.

00:51:15.339 --> 00:51:16.380
It's a very good session.

00:51:19.520 --> 00:51:20.120
So that's it.

00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:23.430
Thank you for your attention.

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:25.220
We have seen this--

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:28.550
the cloud ML Engine and
cloud TPU as products.

00:51:28.550 --> 00:51:30.650
My take away is not
on the products,

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:32.750
but what I want
you to remember is

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:35.230
that if you are
a good developer,

00:51:35.230 --> 00:51:37.140
you can build a
machine-learning model.

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:38.770
There are best practices.

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:40.737
There are Lego
blocks that exists.

00:51:40.737 --> 00:51:42.820
You have to follow where
this is state of the art,

00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:47.150
but then you can piece
those pieces together.

00:51:47.150 --> 00:51:51.010
And if you want-- if you don't
want to build your own models,

00:51:51.010 --> 00:51:53.050
well, we have a set
of pre-trained models.

00:51:53.050 --> 00:51:57.220
And new we also have
this Cloud Auto ML

00:51:57.220 --> 00:51:59.929
product that actually
this morning, it

00:51:59.929 --> 00:52:02.470
was announced that it was not
doing just Vision but also more

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:04.960
things, so I need
to update my slide.

00:52:04.960 --> 00:52:07.990
And with that, you can
just throw your data at it

00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:11.410
and let the system figure out
the architecture of your model.

00:52:11.410 --> 00:52:13.540
That's quite advanced.

00:52:13.540 --> 00:52:18.560
I'm amazed that we can build
this, and I find this really,

00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:21.480
really, really,
really marvelous.

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:27.790
And finally if you want to learn
more about how to build models,

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:31.170
you can check out the other
chapters of the TensorFlow

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:32.910
Without a PhD series.

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:35.160
And all the code and
code labs and all that

00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:36.480
is available on GitHub.

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:39.060
You've got the URL over there.

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:40.440
Thank you very much.

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:46.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

